WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.599 --> 00:00:10.233
<b>This is Black Carbon measured at
Shrewsbury Underdale in October 2025.</b>

2
00:00:10.233 --> 00:00:13.233
<b> It's an urban background site, so traffic, domestic burning</b>

3
00:00:13.233 --> 00:00:17.500
<b>and other combustion sources
in the surrounding area can all contribute.</b>

4
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:20.966
<b>Across a whole month of hourly measurements,
it's difficult to see a clear pattern.</b>

5
00:00:20.966 --> 00:00:26.266
<b>Take a single day, then add the other days
and line them up by time of day.</b>

6
00:00:26.266 --> 00:00:31.633
<b>The measurements vary considerably,
and it's still difficult to identify a consistent pattern.</b>

7
00:00:31.633 --> 00:00:37.000
<b>Average each hour across the month,
and we get a typical daily profile for October.</b>

8
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:39.833
<b>Now add November to March.</b>

9
00:00:39.833 --> 00:00:42.700
<b>Two features stand out.</b>

10
00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:45.533
<b>In the morning, Black Carbon rises around rush hour. </b>

11
00:00:45.533 --> 00:00:49.033
<b>That timing is consistent
with a contribution from road traffic.</b>

12
00:00:49.066 --> 00:00:51.500
<b>The larger feature appears in the evening.</b>

13
00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:57.299
<b>Concentrations rise from late afternoon
and remain elevated well beyond the evening rush hour.</b>

14
00:00:57.299 --> 00:01:00.366
<b>That evening rise, particularly in the colder months,</b>

15
00:01:00.366 --> 00:01:05.599
<b> is consistent with domestic solid-fuel burning
contributing to local air pollution.</b>

16
00:01:05.599 --> 00:01:10.433
<b>And that matters, because wood smoke
contains fine particles that can harm our health,</b>

17
00:01:10.433 --> 00:01:14.133
<b>including the health of people
who don't burn wood themselves.</b>

